window.GAKS_SEARCH = [{"t":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone","i":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone","h":"doc/decisions/ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone.html","s":"accepted","x":"ADR-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase stands alone Decided: 2026-10-14 · George Petrov + Devin Decisions 1. tafbase is its own repository and its own corpus. It does not live inside IKC3. gaks.project.json declares it; the static GAKS bundle of PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 delivers the corpus tools. This is the first real consumer of the foreign-project path. 2. The DSL is JSON, not EDN. The SUT model must be readable and writable from Java and Python with no Clojure dependency — the same reason gaks.project.json replaced the EDN declaration in IKC3. 3. Generate, don't interpret. Emitters write committed, reviewable test files (testlogin.py, LoginJourneyTest.java). A generic runtime interpreter was considered and refused: generated code diffs in a PR are legible; hidden magic is not. 4. Tracker integrations are declared adapters, not a hard dependency. tafbase core runs with no IKC3. The IKC3 adapter (fault reports, repaired-on-merge) is the first sink and the demo loop; JIRA and GitHub Issues adapters are additions of the same kind. 5. The IKC3 tracker is the first SUT, not a component of tafbase. Dogfooding means tests exercise the real platform and file reports into it — not that tafbase embeds it. 6. MIT license. The goals are exposure and adoption; copyleft and dual-licensing are refused. Consequences Doc ids take the taf subproject prefix (PLAN-taf-v1-NNN-NN); numbering is this repository's own. The IKC3 deployment tracker can file tickets for tafbase work only if this corpus is registered as a vault of the deployment — an open question, deferred to the first lane."},{"t":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework","i":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase","h":"doc/plans/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase.html","s":"draft","x":"PLAN-taf-v1-001-01 — tafbase: a DSL First test automation framework Status: in progress tafbase declares a system under test (SUT) once, as data, and generates real test suites from the declaration. Python+Playwright is the first emitter; Java is the second, and the second exists to make the parity claim true. Generated suites report into pluggable tracker adapters; the first adapter is IKC3's fault reports, so a red test files a bug into the very platform the tests exercise. Release note (draft) New: a SUT declared once in JSON runs as a Python+Playwright suite; the same declaration refuses the same broken model the same way. Accepted by: T-taf-001 (parity of refusal, env -i, python3 only) New: a failed generated test files a fault report into a configured tracker through a declared adapter; the IKC3 adapter is the first. Accepted by: T-taf-002 Internal: the Java+Playwright emitter proves the same declaration generates equivalent suites in two languages. Accepted by: T-taf-003 (Python and Java emitters agree on the suite) What this does not do v1 generates static journeys only. Model-based generation — walks of the SUT graph, coverage-directed paths, journal replay — is declared in ADR-taf-v1-001-01 and arrives by lanes, not all at once. Lanes ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1 — the SUT model is JSON The contract: SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model. A SUT declares states, transitions and journeys in one JSON document. The loader validates shape, refusals name the broken clause, and no ambiguous form is guessed — one form only. Release note (draft) New: sut.model.json declares pages, actions and journeys; a broken model names its fault. Accepted by: T-taf-001 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/B-2 — the Python+Playwright emitter From a valid model, taf emit --lang python writes a runnable Playwright+pytest suite: page objects, journey tests, and a manifest mapping each test back to the model element that produced it. Release note (draft) New: one declaration produces a committed, reviewable pytest suite. Accepted by: T-taf-004 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/C-3 — tracker adapters and fault reports A test outcome flows to a declared sink. The IKC3 adapter files a fault report and links it to the test's model element; adapters are a declared kind so JIRA or GitHub Issues are additions, not rewrites. Release note (draft) New: a red test files a fault report into IKC3, named for its model element; the report repairs when the fix lands. Accepted by: T-taf-002 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/D-4 — the Java emitter proves parity --lang java emits the equivalent Playwright-Java + JUnit suite. Parity means: same model, same refusal text, same test names, same journey steps — the discipline measured in PLAN-ikc-v3-079-01 applied to a second axis. Release note (draft) New: the same declaration generates the Java suite; both emitters agree or both refuse. Accepted by: T-taf-003 ▶ Lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/E-5 — the tracker is the first SUT IKC3's tracker is the demo"},{"t":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model","i":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model","h":"doc/specs/SPEC-taf-v1-001-01-the-sut-model.html","s":"draft","x":"SPEC-taf-v1-001-01 — the SUT model Related: PLAN-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase ADR-taf-v1-001-01-tafbase-stands-alone The design input of lane TAF/PLAN-taf-v1-001-01/A-1. This document is the contract: a loader and an emitter are correct together when each refusal and each generated behavior below holds. Purpose A system under test (SUT) is declared once, as one JSON document. The declaration is the model. From it, emitters write runnable suites (Python+Playwright, later Java+Playwright) that follow Playwright's own rules: semantic locators, auto-waiting assertions, one journey per test. The same document is the graph the MBT lanes walk later — states and transitions are first-class from v1, so no model is written twice. The document One file per SUT, sut.model.json by convention. Top level: json { \"sut\": {\"name\": \"ikc3-tracker\", \"base-url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:4855\"}, \"states\": {}, \"elements\": {}, \"actions\": {}, \"journeys\": {}, \"endpoints\": {} } sut name (string, required) names the SUT in refusals and the manifest. base-url (string, required) is where journeys begin. No environment indirection in v1: a different deployment is a different file, or a later lane adds env. states A state is a place a journey can be — a page, a dialog, a verified condition. A state is recognized, not assumed: json \"states\": { \"board\": { \"route\": \"/index.html?island=board\", \"marker\": {\"role\": \"heading\", \"name\": \"Board\"} } } route (optional) is the path under base-url that reaches the state directly. marker (required) is a locator that a generated test asserts visible after every arrival — a state is entered only when the SUT proves it. That one rule is what makes generated tests self-verifying instead of hopeful. elements Named locators, declared once, reused everywhere: json \"elements\": { \"new-ticket-button\": {\"role\": \"button\", \"name\": \"New ticket\"}, \"title-field\": {\"label\": \"Title\"}, \"report-card\": {\"testid\": \"report-card\"}, \"legacy-panel\": {\"css\": \".panel--legacy\"} } THE LOCATOR LADDER is the \"proper use\" clause, encoded, not documented: 1. role + name — the accessibility query, preferred 2. label / text / placeholder — user-visible queries 3. testid — the deliberate escape (data-testid) 4. css / xpath — the last resort, allowed and flagged fragile in the manifest A locator with no usable form is refused :bad-locator. The ladder is the emitter's only ordering; a model does not say which rung a locator sits on, the shape does. actions An action is a step the SUT offers — the transitions of the graph: json \"actions\": { \"move-to-triage\": { \"from\": \"backlog\", \"to\": \"triage\", \"do\": [ {\"click\": \"new-ticket-button\"}, {\"fill\": {\"element\": \"title-field\", \"value\": \"the report\"}}, {\"click\": \"save-button\"}, {\"api\": \"move-ticket\", \"args\": {\"category\": \"triage\"}} ] } } from and to name states and are optional per action, required for the graph to be a graph: an action with both is an edge. do is an ordered list of steps: click / fill / check / select — on a named element navigate — to a "}];
